\section{Fixed settings}{

Random forest: \code{ntree = 500}, \code{mtry = floor(sqrt(p))}, \code{nodesize = 10}.
Gradient boosting: \code{distribution = "bernoulli"}, \code{interaction.depth = 3}, \code{shrinkage = 0.05},
\code{bag.fraction = 0.8}; the number of trees is frozen: when \code{n_trees} is
\code{NULL} it is chosen once by 5-fold cross-validation on the full data (\code{gbm} with
\code{cv.folds = 5}, capped at 3,000 trees, derived seed) and then used unchanged for every seed
and every fold; when \code{n_trees} is given it is used as is.
}

\section{Seeds and parallel runs}{

Per-task seeds are derived in the main process from \code{seed} and the model index, passed to
each worker and applied with \code{\link[withr:with_seed]{withr::with_seed()}}, which restores the random number
generator state afterwards. Results are identical for \code{cores = 1} and \code{cores > 1}.
Parallel runs use \code{\link[parallel:mclapply]{parallel::mclapply()}} on Unix and fall back to a serial run on Windows.
}

\section{The "as MatchIt does it" mode}{

With \code{cross_fit = FALSE} the scores are in-sample predictions for logistic regression and
gradient boosting and out-of-bag predictions for the random forest. This mirrors what
\code{MatchIt::matchit()} returns for \code{distance = "glm"}, \code{"gbm"} and \code{"randomforest"}, so that
applied users can compare with their own object. In-sample tree scores are overfit and
shrink overlap, which is why cross-fitting is the default.
}

\section{Quality gate}{

The models table reports the cross-fitted log-loss, Brier score and c-statistic (rank-based
AUC). \code{passed_logloss_gate} is \code{TRUE} when the log-loss is at most
\code{(1 + quality_tolerance)} times the best model's log-loss. The balance part of the
quality gate (post-match maximum absolute standardized mean difference on main terms at most
0.10) needs the matches and is applied later by the wrapper
\code{propensity_sensitivity()}, not here.
}

